---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The hardest part of exercising more is not the workout, it is showing up day after day. This
      tracker strips the habit down to a single tap: did you move today? A walk, a gym session, a bike
      ride, or ten minutes of stretching all count. Each day you tap, your streak grows, and a month of
      activity fills in on the chart. By keeping the daily bar low and the reward immediate, it carries
      you through the fragile early weeks when a habit is most likely to die.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try The Move Today Tracker →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="why-low-bar">
    <h2>Why a Low Bar Wins <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Ambitious plans fail on the first busy day. An hour at the gym, five times a week, is easy to
      skip the moment life gets in the way, and one skip often becomes many. A tiny daily action is
      different: there is almost no day you cannot fit in some movement, so the chain rarely breaks. Once
      the habit of showing up is solid, adding intensity and structure is easy. The habit comes first,
      the details come later.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="dont-break-the-chain">
    <h2>Do Not Break the Chain <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The streak is the engine. Every logged day extends a visible chain, and once that chain has some
      length you will not want to snap it. This "do not break the chain" effect is one of the most
      reliable behaviour tools there is, precisely because the reward is immediate and concrete while the
      fitness payoff is slow and abstract. The streak bridges the gap, giving you a reason to move today
      even when the long-term goal feels far away.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Today gets grace">
      <p>Your streak will not reset just because you have not logged yet today. Only a completed day with no movement breaks it, so an in-progress day is never held against you.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="how-to-use">
    <h2>Using the Tracker <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Bookmark the page and tap the button once you have moved. That is the whole ritual. The streak and
      the month chart update instantly, and the minus button undoes an accidental tap. You decide what
      counts as movement, so set a definition you can honestly meet on your worst day, not your best. If
      you want to log more than one session, tap again, but a single tap is all the streak needs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Setting the bar too high">
      <p>
        If "movement" secretly means a full workout, you will skip it on hard days and lose the streak.
        Define it as something you can do in ten minutes anywhere, and let the habit grow from there.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Quitting after one miss">
      <p>
        A broken streak is not a failure, it is a Tuesday. One missed day changes nothing about your
        fitness. Restart the chain the next day and aim to beat your longest run.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="Private by design">
      <p>Your activity log is stored only in this browser on this device, never uploaded. Clearing your browser storage erases it, and the whole tracker runs offline.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
